OnUse: You reverently open the case. Three pristine discs await. The extended cut adds the full Sturmbeest hunt sequence and an alternate opening on a dystopian Earth. Someday you'll find a Blu-ray player worthy of this artifact.

Id: desk_note_prototype
Name: Dev Note - Prototype Inheritance
Prototype: item
Tags: desk_drawer_loot
Rarity: uncommon
DescriptionShort: a crumpled {{ e.name }}
DescriptionLong: A handwritten note on yellow legal paper. It reads:
+
+ "PROTOTYPE INHERITANCE SYSTEM
+
+ Entities use prototypical inheritance just like JavaScript objects. A 'vase' entity can inherit from 'glass_object' which inherits from 'object'. When you look up a property like OnAttack, we walk up the prototype chain until we find a non-NULL value.
+
+ This is resolved at QUERY TIME using a recursive CTE in PostgreSQL. The resolve_entity() function walks up to 10 levels of inheritance, returning the first non-NULL value for each property. This means you can override just one handler while inheriting everything else.
+
+ The beauty is DRY definitions - hundreds of vases can inherit the same 'you smash the glass' response from glass_object without repeating it."
OnTouch: The paper is slightly greasy, as if someone was eating chips while writing this.
OnUse: You read it again and feel slightly smarter about database design.

Id: desk_note_recfiles
Name: Dev Note - Recfiles
Prototype: item
Tags: desk_drawer_loot
Rarity: uncommon
DescriptionShort: a folded {{ e.name }}
DescriptionLong: A typed note with coffee stains. It reads:
+
+ "GNU RECUTILS - THE WORLD FORMAT
+
+ All world data lives in .rec files - a plain text database format from GNU. It's human-readable, version-controllable, and has schema validation built in.
+
+ The %rec descriptor defines record types, %key ensures unique IDs, %type validates foreign keys between records, and %mandatory enforces required fields. You can query .rec files directly with recsel, or load them into PostgreSQL.
+
+ Why not JSON or YAML? Because recfiles are designed for records. Multi-line strings use continuation (+), and the format is self-documenting. A world designer can edit mansion.rec in Notepad and the bot validates it on startup.
+
+ The tradeoff: we need a sync step to load recfiles into Postgres. But that's fine - world data doesn't change at runtime."
OnTouch: The coffee stains form a ring, suggesting someone used this as a coaster.
OnUse: You nod approvingly at the elegance of plain text databases.

Id: desk_note_flyweight
Name: Dev Note - Flyweight Pattern
Prototype: item
Tags: desk_drawer_loot
Rarity: uncommon
DescriptionShort: a {{ e.name }} with diagrams
DescriptionLong: A note covered in boxes and arrows. It reads:
+
+ "THE FLYWEIGHT PATTERN
+
+ Entity definitions are stored once in the 'entities' table. Instances are stored separately in 'entity_instances' with just a reference back to the entity.
+
+ This means 50 beer bottles in the fridge share ONE entity definition ('beer') but have 50 instance rows with unique UUIDs. Each instance tracks WHERE it is (room or inventory) but shares all the text, handlers, and properties.
+
+ Memory efficiency? Yes. But more importantly: when you fix a typo in beer's description, all 50 instances update instantly. No need to sync instance data.
+
+ The pattern is named for boxing - a flyweight is the lightest class. Our instances are as light as possible: just an ID, location, and a pointer to the heavyweight definition."
OnTouch: The diagram arrows are surprisingly neat for a handwritten note.
OnUse: You trace the arrows with your finger, following the reference from instance to definition.

Id: desk_note_focus
Name: Dev Note - Focus Context
Prototype: item
Tags: desk_drawer_loot
Rarity: uncommon
DescriptionShort: a sticky {{ e.name }}
DescriptionLong: A Post-it note with tiny handwriting. It reads:
+
+ "FOCUS CONTEXT SYSTEM
+
+ The original design was stateless - containers had no 'opened' state. But opening a chest with 20 items meant a wall of text and no autocomplete help.
+
+ Solution: track what the user is 'focused' on. When a template calls effects.set_focus(), we store that focus in the database. Now autocomplete shows ONLY the container contents, plus an escape option to close it.
+
+ Focus persists across bot restarts (stored in PostgreSQL), clears on room movement or 5-minute timeout, and is per-user. Looking at things doesn't affect focus - only explicit open/close/interact actions do.
+
+ This transforms the UX from 'guess the item name' to 'select from dropdown'."
OnTouch: The adhesive is still slightly sticky.
OnUse: You stick it to your forehead for a moment, absorbing its wisdom.

Id: desk_note_fog
Name: Dev Note - Fog of War
Prototype: item
Tags: desk_drawer_loot
Rarity: uncommon
DescriptionShort: a torn {{ e.name }}
DescriptionLong: A note torn from a spiral notebook. It reads:
+
+ "DISCORD AS FOG OF WAR
+
+ The key insight: Discord channel permissions ARE the fog of war. Each room is a channel. You can only see channels you have view_channel permission for. When you move rooms, we revoke permission on the old channel and grant it on the new one.
+
+ This means users literally cannot see rooms they haven't visited. The Discord UI enforces exploration - no peeking at #secret-vault until you find the entrance.
+
+ Channel topics store room descriptions. Zone categories group rooms. The Discord server IS the game map, rendered natively in the client users already have installed.
+
+ The bot just manages permissions. Discord does the rest."
OnTouch: The torn edge is jagged, ripped out in a hurry.
OnUse: You hold it up and squint at the excited handwriting.

Id: desk_note_inventory
Name: Dev Note - Inventory Forums
Prototype: item
Tags: desk_drawer_loot
Rarity: uncommon
DescriptionShort: a laminated {{ e.name }}
DescriptionLong: A laminated card with bullet points. It reads:
+
+ "INVENTORY AS DISCORD FORUMS
+
+ Each player gets a private forum channel for their inventory. Items become threads. Only you can see your forum.
+
+ Why forums? Threads are perfect for items - each thread has a name (item name), a first post (item description), and can hold conversation (future: item interactions). Forums naturally organize threads by activity.
+
+ The forum name uses Braille encoding of your user ID so it's unique but not human-readable. Discord doesn't normalize Braille characters, avoiding the base62 case-collision bug we hit earlier.
+
+ Pickup creates a thread. Drop deletes it. The database tracks thread IDs for sync. If Discord and DB drift, periodic sync repairs the state."
OnTouch: The lamination is slightly cloudy with age.
OnUse: You flip it over. The back says 'REMEMBER: CASCADE DELETE ON USER.'

Id: desk_note_templates
Name: Dev Note - Jinja2 Templates
Prototype: item
Tags: desk_drawer_loot
Rarity: uncommon
DescriptionShort: a colorful {{ e.name }}
DescriptionLong: A note written in multiple colored pens. It reads:
+
+ "JINJA2 TEMPLATES FOR EVERYTHING
+
+ All text fields - descriptions, handlers, everything - are Jinja2 templates. The context includes 'name' (with Discord italics), 'e' (the resolved entity), 'contents' (child items), and 'user'.
+
+ Templates can call side effects: effects.pickup() moves items to inventory, effects.broadcast() sends public messages, effects.destroy() deletes the instance, effects.grant() creates new items.
+
+ The template returns TEXT to the user. Side effects are collected during rendering and executed AFTER. This means '{{ effects.destroy() }}BOOM!' shows 'BOOM!' then deletes the item.
+
+ Error handling: if a template fails, we fall back to description_long or description_short and append an error note. The game keeps working even with broken templates."
OnTouch: The different colored sections are surprisingly well-organized.
OnUse: You appreciate the rainbow of ink colors. Someone really cared about this note.

Id: desk_note_spawning
Name: Dev Note - Spawning Pools
Prototype: item
Tags: desk_drawer_loot
Rarity: uncommon
DescriptionShort: a damp {{ e.name }}
DescriptionLong: A slightly water-damaged note. It reads:
+
+ "SPAWNING POOL SYSTEM
+
+ Static items are boring. Spawning pools create variety. Each pool has a tag query ('beverage'), a location (room or container), a max count, and a respawn interval.
+
+ When the pool is below max_count and enough time has passed, it queries entities matching the tag and picks one randomly. But not uniformly - rarity weights the selection. Common items (weight 600) appear 60% of the time. Mythic items (weight 1) appear 0.1% of the time.
+
+ The fridge always has drinks, but WHICH drinks varies. Sometimes you get Lone Star (common). Rarely, you find Shiner Bock (rare). This turns mundane container-checking into a mini loot game.
+
+ Instances track their spawning_pool_id so we know what to count."
OnTouch: The paper is wrinkled from water damage but still legible.
OnUse: You calculate the odds of finding a mythic item: 1 in 1000.

Id: desk_note_sync
Name: Dev Note - Sync System
Prototype: item
Tags: desk_drawer_loot
Rarity: uncommon
DescriptionShort: a {{ e.name }} with flowcharts
DescriptionLong: A note covered in flowchart symbols. It reads:
+
+ "THE SYNC SYSTEM
+
+ Everything syncs on startup and every 15 minutes. Zones and rooms sync from .rec files to Postgres to Discord. Entities sync from .rec to Postgres. Permissions sync from Postgres to Discord.
+
+ The key insight: the database is the source of truth for LOCATIONS. Recfiles are the source of truth for DEFINITIONS. Discord is the rendered output.
+
+ If someone deletes a Discord channel, next sync recreates it. If someone edits a channel topic, next sync fixes it. If the database loses track of a forum, next sync recovers it by searching Discord.
+
+ Commands block until the first sync completes. This ensures the visibility cache is populated before anyone tries to move rooms. The system is eventually consistent with a 15-minute maximum drift."
OnTouch: You trace the flowchart arrows from 'Bot Startup' to 'Commands Unblocked'.
OnUse: You appreciate how sync handles every drift scenario you can imagine.

Id: desk_note_resolution
Name: Dev Note - Entity Resolution
Prototype: item
Tags: desk_drawer_loot
Rarity: uncommon
DescriptionShort: a dense {{ e.name }}
DescriptionLong: A note with dense text and no margins. It reads:
+
+ "ENTITY RESOLUTION SERVICE
+
+ Users type partial names. 'tab' should match 'Wooden Table'. 'beer' should match multiple drinks. The resolution service handles this mess.
+
+ For autocomplete, we detect context: Are you in a room? An inventory thread? Typing 'i.' prefix? Have focus on a container? Each context shows different entities.
+
+ Autocomplete values are source-prefixed: 'room:Wooden Table', 'inventory:Beer', 'container:Gold Key'. This makes resolution unambiguous - we know WHERE to look based on the prefix.
+
+ Word-prefix matching: 'tab' matches 'Wooden Table' because 'Table' starts with 'tab'. Exact matches rank higher than prefix matches. Multiple matches trigger disambiguation.
+
+ The complexity is hidden. Users just type and select from dropdowns."
OnTouch: Your eyes glaze over slightly at the density of information.
OnUse: You slowly parse each paragraph, gaining insight into the autocomplete magic.

